window.docContents[183].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-eigenvalues-of-mycielskians":{"contents":"Apart from the eigenvalue d of a d-regular graph (and the Laplacian eigenvalue\n0), Mathlib has no eigenvalues of particular graphs. But we can often find them by\nexhibiting eigenvectors, as Mathlib does for d. As an example we show that\neigenvalues of G give eigenvalues of its Mycielskian mμ(G), scaled by the golden\nratio.\n\nMathlib writes M *ᵥ x (Matrix.mulVec) for the product of a matrix M and a\nvector x, whose entry at i is ∑ j, M i j * x j. So a nonzero vector x is an\neigenvector of M with eigenvalue ξ iff M *ᵥ x = ξ • x.\n\nWe list the vertices of mμ(G) in the order given by their type Option (V ⊕ V):\nfirst the apex none, then the left copies vˡ, then the right copies vʳ. Let\nA be the adjacency matrix of G, and write 1 for the all-ones vector. A left\ncopy vˡ is adjacent to both copies of each neighbour of v, and a right copy\nvʳ is adjacent to the left copies of the neighbours of v and to the apex. So the\nadjacency matrix of mμ(G) has the block form shown in the figure.\n\nThe adjacency matrix of the Mycielskian, applied to a vector with blocks 0, tx and x\n\nNow let x be an eigenvector of A with eigenvalue ξ whose entries sum to zero,\ni.e. x is orthogonal to 1. By the above figure, with A x = ξ x and 1ᵀ x = 0,\nthe vector y with blocks 0, t • x and x is sent to the vector with blocks\n0, (t + 1) • ξ • x and t • ξ • x. So y is an eigenvector with eigenvalue\nt * ξ provided t + 1 = t ^ 2. The roots of this quadratic are the golden ratio\nφ = (1 + √5) / 2 and its conjugate ψ = (1 - √5) / 2. In Mathlib these are\nReal.goldenRatio and Real.goldenConj, with notation φ and ψ in the\ngoldenRatio scope, and Real.goldenRatio_sq and Real.goldenConj_sq say that they\nare these roots. An eigenvalue ξ of G need not have an eigenvector whose\nentries sum to zero, and then the construction says nothing about φ * ξ and\nψ * ξ. For example, the only eigenvectors of K[2] with eigenvalue 1 are the\nconstant vectors, and neither φ nor ψ is an eigenvalue of mμ(K[2]), which is\nthe 5-cycle (see below). In a d-regular graph, however, every eigenvalue other\nthan d has such an eigenvector: eigenvectors of a symmetric matrix with distinct\neigenvalues are orthogonal, and 1 is an eigenvector with eigenvalue d.\n\nIn Lean, if we define y by pattern matching on the three kinds of vertex and mark\nthe definition @[simp], then simp evaluates y at none, vˡ and vʳ\nbut leaves y alone when it isn't applied to a vertex.\n\nThe vector on the vertices of mμ(G) with value 0 at none, t * x v at\nvˡ and x v at vʳ./-- The vector on the vertices of mμ(G) with value 0 at none, t * x v at\nvˡ and x v at vʳ. -/\n@[simp]\ndef mycielskianLift {R : Type*} [Zero R] [Mul R] (t : R) (x : V → R) :\n    Option (V ⊕ V) → R\n  | none => 0\n  | vˡ => t * x v\n  | vʳ => x v\n\n\nThe proof is the computation in the figure, with one case for each kind of vertex.\nSums over Option (V ⊕ V) split in the same order: Fintype.sum_option separates\nthe term at none, and Fintype.sum_sum_type splits the rest into the left and\nright copies. We work over any commutative ring, and with any root t of\nt ^ 2 = t + 1.\n\nIf A *ᵥ x = ξ • x, the entries of x sum to zero and t ^ 2 = t + 1, then\nmycielskianLift t x satisfies the corresponding equation for mμ(G), with\neigenvalue t * ξ.\n\nopen Matrix in\ntheorem adjMatrix_mycielskian_mulVec_mycielskianLift [Fintype V]\n    [DecidableRel G.Adj] {R : Type*} [CommRing R] {t ξ : R} (ht : t ^ 2 = t + 1)\n    {x : V → R} (hx : G.adjMatrix R *ᵥ x = ξ • x) (hsum : ∑ v, x v = 0) :\n    mμ(G).adjMatrix R *ᵥ mycielskianLift t x = (t * ξ) • mycielskianLift t x := by\n  -- the entries of A *ᵥ x and of A *ᵥ (t • x)\n  have hv (v : V) : ∑ u, (if G.Adj v u then x u else 0) = ξ * x v := by\n    simpa [mulVec, dotProduct, adjMatrix_apply] using congrFun hx v\n  have htv (v : V) :\n      ∑ u, (if G.Adj v u then t * x u else 0) = t * (ξ * x v) := by\n    simp_rw [← hv, Finset.mul_sum, mul_ite, mul_zero]\n  -- at a left copy we need t ^ 2 = t + 1\n  have hleft (v : V) : t * (ξ * x v) + ξ * x v = t * ξ * (t * x v) := by\n    -- linear_combination e closes the goal if ring shows it is e rearranged\n    linear_combination -(ξ * x v) * ht\n  ext (_ | v | v) <;> simp_all [mulVec, dotProduct, mul_assoc]\n\n\nSince mycielskianLift t x takes the value x v at vʳ, it is nonzero whenever\nx is.\n\nlemma mycielskianLift_ne_zero {R : Type*} [Zero R] [Mul R] (t : R) {x : V → R}\n    (hx : x ≠ 0) : mycielskianLift t x ≠ 0 := by\n  contrapose hx\n  ext v\n  simpa using congrFun hx vʳ\n\n\nAn eigenvector gives an element of the spectrum. As in Mathlib's proof of\nIsRegularOfDegree.mem_spectrum_adjMatrix, we pass from the matrix to the linear map\nMatrix.toLin' it defines, which has the same spectrum (Matrix.spectrum_toLin'),\nand use the fact that eigenvalues of a linear map lie in its spectrum\n(Module.End.HasEigenvalue.mem_spectrum). Taking t = φ and t = ψ gives the\nfollowing.\n\nIf x ≠ 0 is an eigenvector of A with eigenvalue ξ whose entries sum to zero,\nthen φ * ξ and ψ * ξ are eigenvalues of mμ(G).\n\nopen Matrix in\nopen scoped goldenRatio in\ntheorem mem_spectrum_adjMatrix_mycielskian [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n    [DecidableRel G.Adj] {ξ : ℝ} {x : V → ℝ} (hx0 : x ≠ 0)\n    (hx : G.adjMatrix ℝ *ᵥ x = ξ • x) (hsum : ∑ v, x v = 0) :\n    φ * ξ ∈ spectrum ℝ (mμ(G).adjMatrix ℝ) ∧\n      ψ * ξ ∈ spectrum ℝ (mμ(G).adjMatrix ℝ) := by\n  have key {t : ℝ} (ht : t ^ 2 = t + 1) :\n      t * ξ ∈ spectrum ℝ (mμ(G).adjMatrix ℝ) := by\n    rw [← spectrum_toLin']\n    apply Module.End.HasEigenvalue.mem_spectrum\n    exact Module.End.hasEigenvalue_of_hasEigenvector\n      ⟨Module.End.mem_genEigenspace_one.2 (by\n        simpa using adjMatrix_mycielskian_mulVec_mycielskianLift ht hx hsum),\n       mycielskianLift_ne_zero t hx0⟩\n  exact ⟨key Real.goldenRatio_sq, key Real.goldenConj_sq⟩\n\n\nFor example, K[2] has the eigenvector ![1, -1] with eigenvalue -1, and its\nentries sum to zero. Since mμ(K[2]) is isomorphic to C[5]\n(mycielskianCompleteTwoIsoCycleFive), the 5-cycle has eigenvalues -φ and\n-ψ.\n\nopen Matrix in\nopen scoped goldenRatio in\nexample :\n    -φ ∈ spectrum ℝ (C[5].adjMatrix ℝ) ∧ -ψ ∈ spectrum ℝ (C[5].adjMatrix ℝ) := by\n  have hK : K[2].adjMatrix ℝ *ᵥ ![1, -1] = (-1 : ℝ) • ![1, -1] := by\n    ext i\n    fin_cases i <;> simp [mulVec, dotProduct, Fin.sum_univ_two, adjMatrix_apply]\n  rw [← mycielskianCompleteTwoIsoCycleFive.spectrum_adjMatrix]\n  simpa using mem_spectrum_adjMatrix_mycielskian (by simp) hK (by simp)\n\n\nMathlib's spectrum is a set, so it does not record multiplicities. But for a\nd-regular graph G on n vertices this construction accounts for most of the\nspectrum of mμ(G), with multiplicities. The adjacency matrix of G is symmetric,\nso it has an orthogonal basis of eigenvectors that contains 1, and the other\nn - 1 basis vectors are orthogonal to 1. They give 2 * (n - 1) independent\neigenvectors of mμ(G). The remaining three eigenvectors are constant on the left\ncopies and on the right copies, and their eigenvalues are the roots of\nX ^ 3 - d * X ^ 2 - (d ^ 2 + n) * X + d * n. For G = K[2] we have d = 1 and\nn = 2 so this cubic is (X - 2) * (X ^ 2 + X - 1), whose quadratic factor gives\n-φ and -ψ a second time, so the eigenvalues of C[5] are 2, and -φ and -ψ\neach with multiplicity two. This is a coincidence: for a d-regular G on n\nvertices this happens only when n = 2 * d ^ 2.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Algebraic Graph Theory\u0009The Adjacency Matrix","header":"12.2.2. Eigenvalues of Mycielskians","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-eigenvalues-of-mycielskians"},"/Constructions/#section-exercises-constructions":{"contents":"1. Show that a Cayley graph defined on a commutative additive group is simply a\ncirculant graph. (Hint: addCayley_adj', circulantGraph_adj and\nadd_sub_cancel_left could be useful.)\n\nexample (G : Type*) [AddCommGroup G] (s : Set G) :\n    addCayley s = circulantGraph s := by\n  sorry\n\n\n2. Show that the Paley graph on 17 vertices is a circulant graph with the following\ndifferences. Hint: first show that the set of squares in ZMod 17 is\n{0, 1, 2, 4, 8, 9, 13, 15, 16}. After ext the goal mentions a new variable of\ntype ZMod 17, but decide only works on statements with no free variables. Instead\nuse decide +revert, which first reverts the variable and then checks the resulting\nstatement ∀ y : ZMod 17, … case by case.\n\nlemma paleyGraph₁₇_eq_circulant' :\n    paleyGraph (ZMod 17) = circulantGraph {1, 2, 4, 8, 9, 13, 15, 16} := by\n  sorry\n\n\n3. Prove that in Q[n + 1] the vertex (v, b₁) is adjacent to (v, b₂) if and only\nif b₁ ≠ b₂.\n\nexample {n : ℕ} (v : HypercubeVert n) (b₁ b₂ : Bool) :\n    Q[n + 1].Adj (v, b₁) (v, b₂) ↔ b₁ ≠ b₂ := by\n  sorry\n\n\n4. Prove that the union (i.e. sup ⊔) of the complete bipartite graph between V\nand W with the disjoint union ⊕g (i.e. sum) of complete graphs on V and W\nis the complete graph on V ⊕ W.\n\nexample {V W : Type*} : completeBipartiteGraph V W ⊔ (⊤ ⊕g ⊤) = ⊤ := by\n  sorry\n\n\n5. Prove that the complete 1-partite graph with a single class ℕ is empty. (Hint:\nif i : Fin 1 then fin_cases i splits the goal into one case for each possible\nvalue of i.)\n\nexample : completeMultipartiteFinNat 1 = ⊥ := by\n  sorry\n\n\n6. Prove that the blow-up of a complete graph is a complete multipartite graph.\n\nexample {ι : Type*} (V : ι → Type*) :\n    (⊤ : SimpleGraph ι).blowUpGraph V = completeMultipartiteGraph V := by\n  sorry\n\n\n7. Prove that the cycle graph C[3] is the complete graph on Fin 3. (Mathlib has\nthis as cycleGraph_three_eq_top, but try to prove it directly.)\n\nexample : C[3] = K[3] := by\n  sorry\n\n\n8. Prove that the disjoint union of empty graphs is the empty graph.\n\nexample {ι : Type*} {V : ι → Type*} : Σg (fun i => (⊥ : SimpleGraph (V i))) = ⊥ := by\n  sorry\n\n\n9. Prove that every left copy of a vertex is isolated in the Mycielskian of the\nempty graph. (So mμ(⊥) is a star on the right copies together with the isolated\nleft copies.)\n\nexample {V : Type*} (u : V) (x : Option (V ⊕ V)) :\n    ¬ mμ(⊥).Adj uˡ x := by\n  sorry\n\n\n10. Prove that the cycle graph C[n + 2] is circulantGraph {1}. (Mathlib has\nthis, for C[n + 1], as cycleGraph_eq_circulantGraph, but prove it directly.)\n\nexample (n : ℕ) : C[n + 2] = circulantGraph {1} := by\n  sorry\n\n\n11. Prove that sigmaCompleteNat is the complement of the complete multipartite\ngraph completeMultipartiteNatFin. Hint: after ext ⟨i, a⟩ ⟨j, b⟩, consider the\ncases i = j and i ≠ j separately. In the first case subst the equation, so that\na and b have the same type.\n\nexample : sigmaCompleteNat = completeMultipartiteNatFinᶜ := by\n  sorry\n\n\n1. Show that a Cayley graph defined on a commutative additive group is simply a\ncirculant graph. (Hint: addCayley_adj', circulantGraph_adj and\nadd_sub_cancel_left could be useful.)example (G : Type*) [AddCommGroup G] (s : Set G) :\n    addCayley s = circulantGraph s := by\n  ext u v\n  rw [addCayley_adj', circulantGraph_adj]\n  grind [add_sub_cancel_left]\n2. Show that the Paley graph on 17 vertices is a circulant graph with the following\ndifferences. Hint: first show that the set of squares in ZMod 17 is\n{0, 1, 2, 4, 8, 9, 13, 15, 16}. After ext the goal mentions a new variable of\ntype ZMod 17, but decide only works on statements with no free variables. Instead\nuse decide +revert, which first reverts the variable and then checks the resulting\nstatement ∀ y : ZMod 17, … case by case.lemma paleyGraph₁₇_eq_circulant :\n    paleyGraph (ZMod 17) = circulantGraph {1, 2, 4, 8, 9, 13, 15, 16} := by\n  have : {y : ZMod 17 | IsSquare y} = {0, 1, 2, 4, 8, 9, 13, 15, 16} := by\n    ext x\n    constructor <;> decide +revert\n  simp [this, circulantGraph_eq_erase_zero]\n3. Prove that in Q[n + 1] the vertex (v, b₁) is adjacent to (v, b₂) if and only\nif b₁ ≠ b₂.example {n : ℕ} (v : HypercubeVert n) (b₁ b₂ : Bool) :\n    Q[n + 1].Adj (v, b₁) (v, b₂) ↔ b₁ ≠ b₂ := by simp\n4. Prove that the union (i.e. sup ⊔) of the complete bipartite graph between V\nand W with the disjoint union ⊕g (i.e. sum) of complete graphs on V and W\nis the complete graph on V ⊕ W.example {V W : Type*} : completeBipartiteGraph V W ⊔ (⊤ ⊕g ⊤) = ⊤ := by\n  ext u v\n  cases u <;> cases v <;> simp\n5. Prove that the complete 1-partite graph with a single class ℕ is empty. (Hint:\nif i : Fin 1 then fin_cases i splits the goal into one case for each possible\nvalue of i.)example : completeMultipartiteFinNat 1 = ⊥ := by\n  ext ⟨i, _⟩ ⟨j, _⟩\n  fin_cases i ; fin_cases j; simp\n6. Prove that the blow-up of a complete graph is a complete multipartite graph.example {ι : Type*} (V : ι → Type*) :\n    (⊤ : SimpleGraph ι).blowUpGraph V = completeMultipartiteGraph V := rfl\n7. Prove that the cycle graph C[3] is the complete graph on Fin 3. (Mathlib has\nthis as cycleGraph_three_eq_top, but try to prove it directly.)example : C[3] = K[3] := by\n  ext\n  decide +revert\n8. Prove that the disjoint union of empty graphs is the empty graph.example {ι : Type*} {V : ι → Type*} : Σg (fun i => (⊥ : SimpleGraph (V i))) = ⊥ := by\n  ext ⟨i, u⟩ ⟨j, v⟩\n  simp\n9. Prove that every left copy of a vertex is isolated in the Mycielskian of the\nempty graph. (So mμ(⊥) is a star on the right copies together with the isolated\nleft copies.)example {V : Type*} (u : V) (x : Option (V ⊕ V)) :\n    ¬ mμ(⊥).Adj uˡ x := by\n  rcases x with _ | (v | v) <;> simp\n10. Prove that the cycle graph C[n + 2] is circulantGraph {1}. (Mathlib has\nthis, for C[n + 1], as cycleGraph_eq_circulantGraph, but prove it directly.)example (n : ℕ) : C[n + 2] = circulantGraph {1} := by\n  ext u v\n  grind [cycleGraph_adj, circulantGraph_adj]\n11. Prove that sigmaCompleteNat is the complement of the complete multipartite\ngraph completeMultipartiteNatFin. Hint: after ext ⟨i, a⟩ ⟨j, b⟩, consider the\ncases i = j and i ≠ j separately. In the first case subst the equation, so that\na and b have the same type.example : sigmaCompleteNat = completeMultipartiteNatFinᶜ := by\n  ext ⟨i, a⟩ ⟨j, b⟩\n  by_cases h : i = j\n  · subst h\n    simp\n  · simp [h]\n\n\nend SimpleGraph\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Constructions","header":"3.8. Exercises","id":"/Constructions/#section-exercises-constructions"},"/Distance/#section-distance-invariants-under-isomorphism":{"contents":"Since isomorphisms preserve distances they preserve eccentricities, and hence the\ndiameter and the radius are isomorphism invariants.\n\nlemma Iso.eccent_eq {W : Type*} {H : SimpleGraph W} (e : G ≃g H) (u : V) :\n    H.eccent (e u) = G.eccent u :=\n  (e.toEquiv.iSup_comp (g := H.edist (e u))).symm.trans (iSup_congr (e.edist_eq u))\n\n\nlemma Iso.ediam_eq {W : Type*} {H : SimpleGraph W} (e : G ≃g H) :\n    G.ediam = H.ediam :=\n  (iSup_congr fun u => (e.eccent_eq u).symm).trans\n    (e.toEquiv.iSup_comp (g := H.eccent))\n\n\nlemma Iso.radius_eq {W : Type*} {H : SimpleGraph W} (e : G ≃g H) :\n    G.radius = H.radius :=\n  (iInf_congr fun u => (e.eccent_eq u).symm).trans\n    (e.toEquiv.iInf_comp (g := H.eccent))\n\n\nIn a vertex-transitive graph (IsVertexTransitive) all eccentricities coincide, so\nthe radius equals the diameter and every vertex is central. We use this below to\ncompute the radius of the hypercube from its diameter.\n\nlemma IsVertexTransitive.eccent_eq (h : G.IsVertexTransitive) (u v : V) :\n    G.eccent u = G.eccent v := by\n  obtain ⟨f, hf⟩ := h u v\n  rw [← hf, f.eccent_eq]\n\n\nlemma IsVertexTransitive.radius_eq_ediam [Nonempty V] (h : G.IsVertexTransitive) :\n    G.radius = G.ediam :=\n  radius_eq_ediam_iff.mpr\n    ⟨G.eccent (Classical.arbitrary V), fun u => h.eccent_eq u _⟩\n\n\nlemma IsVertexTransitive.center_eq_univ [Nonempty V] (h : G.IsVertexTransitive) :\n    G.center = Set.univ :=\n  center_eq_univ_iff_radius_eq_ediam.mpr h.radius_eq_ediam\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Distance\u0009Eccentricity, Diameter and Radius","header":"11.2.1. Distance Invariants under Isomorphism","id":"/Distance/#section-distance-invariants-under-isomorphism"},"/Introduction/#section-installation":{"contents":"If you haven't done so already please install Lean\nand clone a copy of the repository https://github.com/jt496/GraphTheoryInLean.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Introduction","header":"1.1. Installation","id":"/Introduction/#section-installation"},"/Maps/#section-comaps":{"contents":"Given a function f : V → W, we can push a graph G : SimpleGraph V forwards along\nf or pull a graph H : SimpleGraph W back along f. The former is called the\nmap of G along f and the latter is called the comap of H along f.\n\nOf these, SimpleGraph.comap is used frequently in constructions of concrete graphs,\nso we start with it. (The SimpleGraph.map construction is less frequently used for\nconstructing concrete graphs, but often appears in proofs; we return to it in\nMaps, once we have met graph homomorphisms.)\n\nGiven f : V → W and G : SimpleGraph W, G.comap f is the SimpleGraph V where\ntwo vertices are adjacent iff their images under f are adjacent in G.\n\nG (right) pulled back back along f : V \\to W (dashed arrows), to give G.comap f (left)\n\n#source SimpleGraph.comap\nexample {V W : Type*} (f : V → W) {v₁ v₂ : V} (G : SimpleGraph W) :\n  (G.comap f).Adj v₁ v₂ ↔ G.Adj (f v₁) (f v₂) := comap_adj ..\n\n\nTwo examples of graphs defined using SimpleGraph.comap are complete multipartite\n(completeMultipartiteGraph)and blow-up graphs (blowUpGraph). (If you read the\nsection Complete Multipartite Graphs and Blow-ups, you have\nalready seen them.) Two vertices are adjacent in a complete multipartite graph iff\nthey are in different parts.\n\n#source completeMultipartiteGraph    -- SimpleGraph (Σ i, V i) := .comap Sigma.fst ⊤\nexample {ι : Type*} (V : ι → Type*) {u v : Σ i, V i} :\n    (completeMultipartiteGraph V).Adj u v ↔ u.fst ≠ v.fst := Iff.rfl\n\n\nTwo vertices are adjacent in the blow-up of a graph G iff they are in parts that\nare adjacent in G.\n\n#source blowUpGraph -- abbrev blowUpGraph {ι : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph ι)\n                    --    (V : ι → Type*) : SimpleGraph (Σ i, V i) :=\n                    --                          SimpleGraph.comap Sigma.fst G\n\n\nexample {ι : Type*} (V : ι → Type*) {u v : Σ i, V i} (G : SimpleGraph ι) :\n    (G.blowUpGraph V).Adj u v ↔ G.Adj u.fst v.fst := Iff.rfl\n\n\nIn both cases the adjacency relation is defined by pulling back the adjacency\nrelation of a graph using SimpleGraph.comap and the map Sigma.fst : Σ i, V i → ι\nthat sends a vertex to its part.\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Maps","header":"5.2. Comaps","id":"/Maps/#section-comaps"},"/Maps/#section-copies":{"contents":"We already met graph homomorphisms A →g B and graph embeddings A ↪g B. Copy A B\nfalls between the two: it is the type of injective graph homomorphisms from A to\nB.\n\nWhile A ↪g B captures the type of induced copies of a graph A in a graph B,\nCopy A B captures the type of not-necessarily-induced copies of A in B.\n\n#source Copy\n\n\nNote that the copies are labelled: an element of Copy A B records where each\nvertex of A goes. So if A has nontrivial automorphisms then a single subgraph of\nB isomorphic to A is the image of several elements of Copy A B, one for each\nautomorphism of A. We return to this distinction in\nCounting Copies.\n\nAn induced copy of A in B (i.e. f : A ↪g B) gives rise to a copy of A in\nB.\n\n#source Embedding.toCopy -- .. (f : A ↪g B) : Copy A B := f.toHom.toCopy f.injective\n\n\nNote that any copy of a complete graph is necessarily induced, so we can always\nupgrade a Copy ⊤ G to a graph embedding ⊤ ↪g G.\n\nexample {V W : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V} (c : Copy (⊤ : SimpleGraph W) G) :\n    (⊤ : SimpleGraph W) ↪g G := c.topEmbedding\n\n\nWe say that A is contained in B, written A.IsContained B or A ⊑ B, iff\nthere exists a Copy A B.\n\n#source IsContained -- (A : SimpleGraph α) (B : SimpleGraph β) := Nonempty (Copy A B)\n\n\nIf B contains no copy of A then we say that B is A-free, written\nA.Free B. This follows the convention of extremal graph theory, where \"A-free\"\nmeans containing no copy of A at all (as in \"triangle-free\"). Beware that\nstructural graph theory often uses \"A-free\" to mean containing no induced copy of\nA (as in \"claw-free\"). For that notion we need a separate definition, which we give\nin Claw-Free Graphs.\n\n#source Free -- abbrev Free (A : SimpleGraph α) (B : SimpleGraph β) := ¬A ⊑ B\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Maps\u0009Morphisms","header":"5.3.3. Copies","id":"/Maps/#section-copies"},"/Subgraphs/#section-matching-number-of-the-hypercube":{"contents":"The hypercube Q[n + 1] has a natural perfect matching: pair each vertex with the\none obtained by flipping its first coordinate, so v[0, …] is matched with\nv[1, …]. (Flipping the first coordinate rather than the last lets us build the\nmatching by recursion on n.)\n\nRather than reason about the hypercube directly, we isolate the underlying general\nfact about box products: if M is a perfect matching of G, then copying M into\nevery G-fibre of G □ H is a perfect matching of G □ H, for any graph H, whose\nedges play no role at all.\n\nCopy a subgraph M of G into every G-fibre of G □ H: the vertex (v, b)\nis present iff v ∈ M.verts, and (a, b) and (c, b) are adjacent iff a and c\nare adjacent in M. This is the box product of M with the edgeless (but fully\nspanning) subgraph on H./-- Copy a subgraph M of G into every G-fibre of G □ H: the vertex (v, b)\nis present iff v ∈ M.verts, and (a, b) and (c, b) are adjacent iff a and c\nare adjacent in M. This is the box product of M with the edgeless (but fully\nspanning) subgraph on H. -/\n@[simps]\ndef Subgraph.boxProdLeft {β : Type*} (M : G.Subgraph) (H : SimpleGraph β) :\n    (G □ H).Subgraph where\n  verts := {x | x.1 ∈ M.verts}\n  Adj x y := M.Adj x.1 y.1 ∧ x.2 = y.2\n  adj_sub h := boxProd_adj.2 (Or.inl ⟨M.adj_sub h.1, h.2⟩)\n  edge_vert h := M.edge_vert h.1\n  symm := ⟨fun _ _ ⟨h1, h2⟩ => ⟨h1.symm, h2.symm⟩⟩\n\n\nIf M is a perfect matching of G then M.boxProdLeft H is a perfect matching of\nG □ H: the unique neighbour of (v, b) is (w, b), where w is the M-partner\nof v.\n\nlemma Subgraph.IsPerfectMatching.boxProdLeft {β : Type*} {M : G.Subgraph}\n    (h : M.IsPerfectMatching) (H : SimpleGraph β) :\n    (M.boxProdLeft H).IsPerfectMatching := by\n  rw [Subgraph.isPerfectMatching_iff]\n  rintro ⟨v, b⟩\n  obtain ⟨w, hw, huniq⟩ := h.1 (h.2 v)\n  refine ⟨(w, b), ⟨hw, rfl⟩, ?_⟩\n  rintro ⟨c, d⟩ ⟨hadj, rfl⟩\n  exact Prod.ext (huniq c hadj) rfl\n\n\nA graph, or rather its top subgraph, is a perfect matching iff every vertex has\ndegree one.\n\nlemma Subgraph.isPerfectMatching_top_iff [G.LocallyFinite] :\n    (⊤ : G.Subgraph).IsPerfectMatching ↔ ∀ v : V, G.degree v = 1 := by\n  simp [isPerfectMatching_iff, SimpleGraph.degree_eq_one_iff_existsUnique_adj]\n\nend matchingnumber\n\n\nThe recursive perfect matching of the hypercube is now immediate.\n\nA perfect matching of Q[n + 1] defined recursively: ⊤ on Q[1], and on\nQ[n + 2] the Subgraph.boxProdLeft of hypercube.matching n along K₂./-- A perfect matching of Q[n + 1] defined recursively: ⊤ on Q[1], and on\nQ[n + 2] the Subgraph.boxProdLeft of hypercube.matching n along K₂. -/\ndef hypercube.matching : (n : ℕ) → Q[n + 1].Subgraph\n  | 0 => ⊤\n  | n + 1 => (hypercube.matching n).boxProdLeft ⊤\n\n\nBy induction hypercube.matching n is a perfect matching of Q[n + 1].\n\nlemma hypercube.isPerfectMatching_matching :\n    ∀ n : ℕ, (hypercube.matching n).IsPerfectMatching\n  | 0 => Subgraph.isPerfectMatching_top_iff.mpr <| IsRegularOfDegree.hypercube _\n  | n + 1 => (hypercube.isPerfectMatching_matching n).boxProdLeft ⊤\n\n\nSince Q[n + 1] has 2 ^ (n + 1) vertices, this perfect matching gives\nν Q[n + 1] = 2 ^ n.\n\nlemma matchingNum_hypercube_succ (n : ℕ) : ν Q[n + 1] = 2 ^ n := by\n  rw [(hypercube.isPerfectMatching_matching n).matchingNum_eq]\n  have h : Nat.card (HypercubeVert (n + 1)) / 2 = 2 ^ n := by\n    rw [Nat.card_eq_fintype_card, card_hypercubeVert, pow_succ]; grind\n  exact_mod_cast h\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Subgraphs\u0009Matching Number","header":"9.5.1. Matching Number of the Hypercube","id":"/Subgraphs/#section-matching-number-of-the-hypercube"}});